{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_546474979#4_989936053","relevance":1,"reason":"This is an astrology transit article that says a Sun-conjunct-Moon interpretation can also be read for a solar eclipse and mentions emotions and psychological self-analysis. It is related only through astrology and does not provide reliable direct evidence on solar eclipses affecting mental health.","learned":"Astrology transit pages may connect solar eclipses with emotions or psychological self-analysis, but they are not strong evidence for actual mental-health effects."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"solar eclipse\" (+ suicide suicides depression psychiatric \"mental illness\"))","reason":"Search for psychiatric or epidemiological evidence on serious mental-health outcomes such as suicide or depression around solar eclipses, which may produce more direct, evidence-based grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"solar eclipse\" (+ suicide suicides depression psychiatric \"mental illness\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_429207581#15_752093852","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2679","rank":1,"snippet":"1999 by Mark Littmann, Ken Willcox and Fred Espenak Index to Observing Eclipses Safely Introduction WARNING! The Pinhole Projection Method Solar Filters Welders' Goggles Camera and Telescope Solar Filters Fully Exposed and Developed Black-and-White Film Eye Suicide Observing with Binoculars Observing with a Telescope A Final Thought Copyright ©1999 by Mark Littmann, Ken Willcox and Fred Espenak For more information, see: Totality - Eclipses of the Sun Eclipse Resources Solar Eclipses and Eye Safety Solar Eclipse Predictions Solar Eclipse Photography Solar Eclipse Photographs<cover>Solar Eclipse Reports and Photographs Other Links Copyright Notice Return to: Totality - Eclipses of the Sun Return to: MrEclipse Main Page \" , \"segment\": \"Eye Suicide</cover>Standard or polaroid sunglasses are not solar filters. They may afford some eye relief if you are outside on a bright day, but you would never think of using them to stare at the Sun. So you cannot use sunglasses, even crossed polaroids, to stare at the Sun during the partial phases of an eclipse. They provide little or no eye protection for this purpose. Observing with Binoculars Binoculars were astronomy writer George Lovi's favorite instrument for observing total eclipses. Any size will do. "}}
