{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ eclipse (+ awe humble humility connectedness prosocial) (+ twitter tweets study research))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_661076552#1_1189675405","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2694","rank":1,"snippet":"dropped six degrees, flowers closed their petals, dogs howled, pigeons tucked their heads under their wings as if to sleep and the whole city was bathed in a kind of diffused light, not unlike that accompanying the approach of a severe storm. As the light from the Sun passed through the leaves of trees, it projected on to the sidewalk pavement tiny wedgelike images of its own crescent silhouette. For most, the eclipse provided a brief moment of celestial entertainment, a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity to witness a phenomenon that since the beginning of history has inspired <cover>awe, fear and fascination. But scientists said they also hoped that detailed study of the eclipse </cover>would lend new data for a variety of astronomical calculations, ranging from judging the height of the Moon's mountains to measuring the diameter of the Sun.\" , \"start_char\": 1044.000000 , \"title\": \"MILLIONS IN SOUTH TREATED TO RARE VIEW OF SOLAR ECLIPSE - The New York Times\" , \"url\": \"http://www.nytimes.com/1984/05/31/us/millions-in-south-treated-to-rare-view-of-solar-eclipse.html\" }"}}
