{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_1071220674#1_2364100615","relevance":2,"reason":"This travel/advice article describes the total solar eclipse experience as alluring tourists with awe and splendor and gives sensory details of totality. It supports an emotional/psychological response, but it is not dedicated to mental health effects.","learned":"Total solar eclipses are commonly described as awe-inspiring experiences with powerful sensory and emotional impact, though travel articles do not focus on mental health."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"total solar eclipse\" (+ emotional emotions \"emotional impact\" \"emotional experience\" \"life-changing\" awe) (+ psychological mental experience))","reason":"Search for documents specifically describing the emotional or psychological experience of a total solar eclipse, with terms like life-changing and emotional impact that may yield stronger mental-health-relevant evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"total solar eclipse\" (+ emotional emotions \"emotional impact\" \"emotional experience\" \"life-changing\" awe) (+ psychological mental experience))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_429119659#9_1028263445","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2701","rank":1,"snippet":"how to build your own here. Stand under a tree and look at the ground. The trees act as pinhole projectors and will project hundreds of crescent shapes right at your feet. To capture an eclipse with binoculars, a telescope, or a camera, you must use a safety-approved, protective solar filter on your lens. Keeping with the theme of 2020 – Observe the eclipse virtually! It will be streamed live here. NASA Marshall Experts to Share Total Solar Eclipse In-person, on TV On Monday, Aug. 21, for the first time in almost 100 years, all of North America will be treated to an eclipse of the sun. Those in the path of totality, running from Oregon to South Carolina, will <cover>experience one of nature’s most awe-inspiring events — a total solar eclipse. </cover>Scientists, researchers and experts from NASA’s Marshall Space Flight Center in Huntsville, Alabama, will mobilize to experience the eclipse and share it with others.\" , \"start_char\": 4629.000000 , \"title\": \"solar eclipse – Watch the Skies\" , \"url\": \"https://blogs.nasa.gov/Watch_the_Skies/tag/solar-eclipse/\" }"}}
